
  [1mThe fork:[0m all three store tasks in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m and compute due/overdue the same way. What differs is how the code is
  split, and that determines whether you can open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it.

  [1mA. Single file, vanilla JS[0m — everything inline in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. State array in memory, serialized to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m on
  every mutation, full re-render of [38;5;153m<main>[39m on change.
  - Buys: zero tooling, zero dependencies, works from [38;5;153mfile://[39m by double-click.
  - Costs: one file that grows into storage + rendering + date logic tangled together. Testing means testing through the
    DOM.
  - Rules out: clean unit tests on the date logic, which is the one part with real edge cases.

  [1mB. ES modules, no build step[0m — [38;5;153mstore.js[39m (load/save/mutate), [38;5;153mreminders.js[39m (pure date→status functions), [38;5;153mui.js[39m (render),
  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m as the shell.
  - Buys: the due/overdue logic becomes pure functions you can test directly; each file has one job and stays small.
  - Costs: native ES modules are blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m by CORS, so you need a one-line dev server ([38;5;153mpython3 -m [39m
    [38;5;153mhttp.server[39m) to open it. That's the real price.
  - Rules out: nothing meaningful.

  [1mC. Framework (Preact/React via CDN or Vite)[0m — component tree, reactive re-render.
  - Buys: rendering handled for you; scales if this grows.
  - Costs: a dependency and probably a build step for a list with an add form and a date comparison.
  - Rules out: nothing, but it's tooling weight ahead of demand.

  [1mRecommendation: B.[0m The only part of this app with genuine bugs hiding in it is the date logic — timezones, "due today"
  at 11pm, overdue-vs-due-now boundaries, what happens when a due date passes while the tab sits open. Approach B makes
  that a pure function with no DOM and no storage attached, which means you can test it properly. A buries it in a
  script tag; C adds a dependency to solve a rendering problem you don't have. The dev-server requirement is B's only
  real cost, and it's one command.

  I'll also flag: you have no tooling configured at all, so once you pick, I'll ask a short tooling question
  (lint/format, unit tests, e2e) as part of the design — it's cheapest to decide before any code exists.

  Which approach do you want?

